1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,701
You're a hapless programmer.

2
00:00:01,701 --> 00:00:04,521
Right now, you're staring at a frustrating internal document,

3
00:00:04,521 --> 00:00:06,756
coding an even more frustrating program.

4
00:00:06,756 --> 00:00:07,557
Unsurprisingly,

5
00:00:07,557 --> 00:00:09,325
you soon run into a problem.

6
00:00:09,325 --> 00:00:10,727
You ask the large model,

7
00:00:10,727 --> 00:00:13,496
but it turns out it's never seen your document before.

8
00:00:13,563 --> 00:00:16,366
So, it starts confidently spouting nonsense.

9
00:00:16,366 --> 00:00:17,734
Its answers sound perfectly reasonable,

10
00:00:17,734 --> 00:00:20,270
but they have absolutely nothing to do with your question.

11
00:00:20,270 --> 00:00:23,289
People call this 'model hallucination'.

12
00:00:23,289 --> 00:00:25,091
But you're a smart programmer,

13
00:00:25,091 --> 00:00:27,560
so you quickly figure out a solution:

14
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:30,230
send the document and the question to the model together.

15
00:00:30,230 --> 00:00:32,732
This time, the AI gives the correct answer.

16
00:00:32,732 --> 00:00:34,167
Problem solved, it seems.

17
00:00:34,167 --> 00:00:36,703
But soon, new problems arise.

18
00:00:36,703 --> 00:00:38,588
Your document gets larger and larger,

19
00:00:38,588 --> 00:00:42,192
and the answer might be hidden in just a small section of it,

20
00:00:42,192 --> 00:00:44,627
or even in scattered fragments.

21
00:00:44,627 --> 00:00:47,897
When the AI sees the entire document, it actually struggles to find the key information.

22
00:00:47,964 --> 00:00:49,332
Basically, with too much information,

23
00:00:49,332 --> 00:00:50,967
the model can easily get sidetracked.

24
00:00:50,967 --> 00:00:52,902
Being smart, you quickly think,

25
00:00:52,902 --> 00:00:55,622
"What if I don't send the entire document?"

26
00:00:55,622 --> 00:00:59,359
"And only send the parts that are truly relevant to the question?"

27
00:00:59,359 --> 00:01:00,360
Of course, you can!

28
00:01:00,627 --> 00:01:05,365
This is exactly what RAG (Retrieval Augmented Generation) aims to solve.

29
00:01:05,365 --> 00:01:06,433
So, the question is,

30
00:01:06,433 --> 00:01:09,736
how do we determine if a piece of text

31
00:01:09,736 --> 00:01:10,987
is relevant to the user's question?

32
00:01:10,987 --> 00:01:12,722
We need a new kind of model,

33
00:01:12,722 --> 00:01:14,157
called an Embedding model.

34
00:01:14,190 --> 00:01:17,026
An Embedding model also takes a piece of text as input,

35
00:01:17,026 --> 00:01:18,628
but unlike Large Language Models,

36
00:01:18,728 --> 00:01:21,564
its output is a fixed-length array.

37
00:01:21,564 --> 00:01:25,168
For instance, OpenAI's text-embedding-3-small model

38
00:01:25,168 --> 00:01:28,421
outputs a 1536-dimensional array,

39
00:01:28,421 --> 00:01:31,024
while the text-embedding-3-large model

40
00:01:31,024 --> 00:01:33,760
outputs a 3072-dimensional array.

41
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:34,461
In this case,

42
00:01:34,461 --> 00:01:37,931
whether you input a whole sentence or an entire paragraph,

43
00:01:37,931 --> 00:01:41,034
the length of the array output by the model is always fixed.

44
00:01:41,034 --> 00:01:42,969
You can think of this array as

45
00:01:43,153 --> 00:01:45,388
a lossy compression of the original content.

46
00:01:45,388 --> 00:01:46,756
The information is condensed,

47
00:01:46,790 --> 00:01:48,324
but the meaning is still preserved.

48
00:01:48,324 --> 00:01:52,162
The compressed results of similar content will also be very close to each other.

49
00:01:52,228 --> 00:01:55,365
So, we can use the distance between these arrays

50
00:01:55,365 --> 00:01:58,101
to determine if two pieces of text are related.

51
00:01:58,101 --> 00:01:59,269
For ease of explanation,

52
00:01:59,269 --> 00:02:00,203
let's draw a

53
00:02:00,203 --> 00:02:03,523
coordinate system with the same number of dimensions as the array length.

54
00:02:03,523 --> 00:02:04,991
It seems Wang can't draw a

55
00:02:04,991 --> 00:02:07,060
1536-dimensional coordinate system.

56
00:02:07,060 --> 00:02:07,494
Alright,

57
00:02:07,594 --> 00:02:11,197
so let's assume we're using a really crappy Embedding model

58
00:02:11,231 --> 00:02:14,134
whose output array only contains two numbers.

59
00:02:14,134 --> 00:02:17,170
Then we can draw a 2D coordinate system.

60
00:02:17,170 --> 00:02:20,557
Each piece of text occupies a point in this coordinate system.

61
00:02:20,557 --> 00:02:24,294
And the Embedding model will try to place texts with similar meanings

62
00:02:24,294 --> 00:02:25,895
closer to each other.

63
00:02:25,895 --> 00:02:27,597
For example, "Wang loves to eat melons"

64
00:02:27,597 --> 00:02:30,099
and "Wang likes eating melons" mean roughly the same thing,

65
00:02:30,233 --> 00:02:32,702
so their positions will be very close.

66
00:02:32,702 --> 00:02:35,522
"I also love Python" would be a bit further away.

67
00:02:35,522 --> 00:02:37,423
And "The plane I just bought got shot down"

68
00:02:37,457 --> 00:02:39,993
would land very, very far away.

69
00:02:39,993 --> 00:02:40,627
Of course,

70
00:02:40,627 --> 00:02:43,963
a 2D coordinate system can't actually accurately represent

71
00:02:43,963 --> 00:02:45,398
all the distance relationships.

72
00:02:45,398 --> 00:02:49,169
This chart would quickly get crammed full of all sorts of sentences,

73
00:02:49,235 --> 00:02:50,270
making it completely unusable.

74
00:02:50,270 --> 00:02:51,487
So in reality,

75
00:02:51,487 --> 00:02:54,891
the actual vector space for Embedding models is over 1000 dimensions,

76
00:02:54,891 --> 00:02:56,860
or even a 3000+ dimensional coordinate system.

77
00:02:56,860 --> 00:02:58,628
In such a high-dimensional space,

78
00:02:58,628 --> 00:03:01,798
the distance relationships between sentences can be expressed much more accurately.

79
00:03:01,798 --> 00:03:03,166
With this vector space,

80
00:03:03,166 --> 00:03:05,869
if a user then asks a question,

81
00:03:05,869 --> 00:03:07,587
like "What does Wang love to eat?"

82
00:03:07,587 --> 00:03:10,890
we can feed this question to the same Embedding model

83
00:03:10,957 --> 00:03:11,824
for processing.

84
00:03:11,858 --> 00:03:14,827
It too will be mapped to a point in the coordinate system.

85
00:03:14,827 --> 00:03:18,264
The Embedding model will place a question like "What does Wang love to eat?"

86
00:03:18,264 --> 00:03:20,033
close to semantic fragments like "Wang loves to eat melons"

87
00:03:20,033 --> 00:03:22,769
or "Wang likes eating melons."

88
00:03:22,769 --> 00:03:24,787
The program can then calculate

89
00:03:24,787 --> 00:03:28,191
the distance between each existing point and this question point,

90
00:03:28,191 --> 00:03:31,327
and then select the few pieces of text that are closest.

91
00:03:31,327 --> 00:03:32,462
These serve as context,

92
00:03:32,462 --> 00:03:35,598
and are sent along with the user's question to the AI model.

93
00:03:35,598 --> 00:03:37,934
This way, the AI model sees only

94
00:03:38,101 --> 00:03:40,136
content that is highly relevant to the question.

95
00:03:40,136 --> 00:03:42,655
The risk of hallucination is thus greatly reduced.

96
00:03:42,655 --> 00:03:44,190
This architecture is RAG.

97
00:03:44,190 --> 00:03:46,125
Let's go back to our earlier problem:

98
00:03:46,226 --> 00:03:47,927
What if the document is too long?

99
00:03:47,927 --> 00:03:49,896
Before the user even asks a question,

100
00:03:49,896 --> 00:03:51,698
we can preprocess the document.

101
00:03:51,698 --> 00:03:52,832
How do we do that?

102
00:03:52,832 --> 00:03:56,536
First, we split the entire document into many small chunks.

103
00:03:56,536 --> 00:03:58,354
There are many ways to do this:

104
00:03:58,354 --> 00:04:03,259
by character count, by paragraph, by sentence,

105
00:04:03,259 --> 00:04:05,695
and of course, more complex methods.

106
00:04:05,695 --> 00:04:07,830
This process has a special name,

107
00:04:07,830 --> 00:04:09,065
which sounds very fancy:

108
00:04:09,065 --> 00:04:10,066
"Chunking."

109
00:04:10,066 --> 00:04:12,035
It just means cutting into pieces.

110
00:04:12,035 --> 00:04:13,102
After chunking,

111
00:04:13,102 --> 00:04:14,621
the next step is to

112
00:04:14,621 --> 00:04:17,423
perform Embedding on each small piece of text,

113
00:04:17,423 --> 00:04:19,959
turning them into arrays of uniform length,

114
00:04:19,959 --> 00:04:22,061
or, more technically, vectors.

115
00:04:22,061 --> 00:04:24,264
But just having these vectors isn't enough.

116
00:04:24,264 --> 00:04:29,235
We also need to store the mapping between each vector and its original text fragment.

117
00:04:29,235 --> 00:04:32,121
Usually, we use a database to store data.

118
00:04:32,388 --> 00:04:33,957
But traditional databases excel at

119
00:04:33,957 --> 00:04:36,426
finding records based on exact numerical values,

120
00:04:36,426 --> 00:04:38,895
and these values aren't vectors.

121
00:04:41,864 --> 00:04:43,066
(blank)

122
00:04:38,895 --> 00:04:41,864
But now, we're not looking for exact matches like "equals X,"

123
00:04:43,066 --> 00:04:45,401
but rather which vector is closest to the vector

124
00:04:45,401 --> 00:04:46,869
corresponding to our question.

125
00:04:46,869 --> 00:04:49,756
This isn't something traditional databases can do.

126
00:04:50,890 --> 00:04:53,159
(blank)

127
00:04:49,756 --> 00:04:50,890
So, something was designed specifically for this scenario:

128
00:04:53,192 --> 00:04:54,761
the vector database.

129
00:04:54,761 --> 00:04:55,995
In a vector database,

130
00:04:55,995 --> 00:04:59,632
each piece of data can have an associated vector.

131
00:04:59,632 --> 00:05:00,566
When you query,

132
00:05:00,566 --> 00:05:04,654
you input a vector, and the database finds the data

133
00:05:04,654 --> 00:05:06,723
whose vectors are closest to your input vector.

134
00:05:06,789 --> 00:05:11,094
This feature is perfect for storing Embedding results.

135
00:05:11,294 --> 00:05:14,831
Common vector databases include Pinecone, ChromaDB,

136
00:05:14,831 --> 00:05:18,267
and combinations like PostgreSQL with the pgvector plugin,

137
00:05:18,267 --> 00:05:20,370
among others.

138
00:05:20,370 --> 00:05:22,789
So, we've completed the document chunking,

139
00:05:22,789 --> 00:05:24,657
encoding, and storage.

140
00:05:24,657 --> 00:05:28,227
All the chunks and their corresponding Embeddings are now ready

141
00:05:28,227 --> 00:05:29,729
to be retrieved.

142
00:05:29,729 --> 00:05:31,497
When a user asks a question,

143
00:05:31,497 --> 00:05:33,900
we first use the same Embedding model

144
00:05:33,933 --> 00:05:36,102
to convert the question into a vector as well.

145
00:05:36,102 --> 00:05:38,154
Then, from the vector database,

146
00:05:38,154 --> 00:05:40,356
we retrieve the few pieces of content that are closest in distance.

147
00:05:40,356 --> 00:05:43,793
These are then sent, along with the user's question, to the AI model.

148
00:05:43,893 --> 00:05:46,996
And there you have it – a complete RAG architecture.

149
00:05:46,996 --> 00:05:49,499
However, RAG itself isn't

150
00:05:49,499 --> 00:05:50,833
a perfect architecture.

151
00:05:50,833 --> 00:05:52,668
It has some inherent flaws.

152
00:05:52,668 --> 00:05:55,421
First, there's the issue of how to chunk articles.

153
00:05:55,421 --> 00:05:58,725
Because the structure and sequence of every article are different,

154
00:05:58,725 --> 00:06:01,294
whether you chunk by sentence, by paragraph,

155
00:06:01,294 --> 00:06:03,629
or use more complex chunking algorithms,

156
00:06:03,796 --> 00:06:05,198
when articles get complex,

157
00:06:05,198 --> 00:06:07,834
no single method can adapt to all scenarios.

158
00:06:07,834 --> 00:06:09,836
Some key content within an article

159
00:06:09,836 --> 00:06:11,988
can sometimes be awkwardly cut off.

160
00:06:11,988 --> 00:06:14,057
For instance, "I am programmer Wang.

161
00:06:14,057 --> 00:06:15,224
I love eating melons."

162
00:06:15,224 --> 00:06:18,227
Here, the 'I' in the second part actually refers to Wang.

163
00:06:18,261 --> 00:06:21,097
But if this passage is split into two,

164
00:06:21,097 --> 00:06:23,266
then the 'I' in the second part loses

165
00:06:23,266 --> 00:06:24,934
its referential connection to Wang.

166
00:06:24,934 --> 00:06:28,154
At this point, if someone asks, "What does Wang like to eat?"

167
00:06:28,221 --> 00:06:31,557
the vector distance between that question and "I love eating melons"

168
00:06:31,557 --> 00:06:32,658
might increase.

169
00:06:32,759 --> 00:06:36,763
Even the most sophisticated chunking strategies can only minimize such misinterpretations,

170
00:06:36,763 --> 00:06:38,598
they can't completely avoid them.

171
00:06:38,598 --> 00:06:41,834
The second problem is that RAG lacks a global perspective.

172
00:06:41,834 --> 00:06:46,322
For example, if a user asks, "How many times does the character 'I' appear in this article?"

173
00:06:46,322 --> 00:06:49,425
This question is actually related to every sentence in the article,

174
00:06:50,193 --> 00:06:51,928
but not strongly related to any particular one.

175
00:06:51,928 --> 00:06:54,130
For questions where no single sentence is especially relevant,

176
00:06:54,130 --> 00:06:56,499
but the whole text is somewhat relevant,

177
00:06:56,499 --> 00:06:58,601
RAG generally can't handle them.

178
00:06:58,601 --> 00:07:00,069
To address these shortcomings,

179
00:07:00,069 --> 00:07:02,655
many improved solutions have emerged.

180
00:07:02,655 --> 00:07:04,190
For example, during preprocessing,

181
00:07:04,190 --> 00:07:06,359
uniformly replacing all instances of "I" with "Wang,"

182
00:07:06,359 --> 00:07:09,896
or even involving a large model in the chunking process

183
00:07:09,896 --> 00:07:12,932
to automatically determine where to break

184
00:07:12,932 --> 00:07:13,866
and how to cut based on semantics.

185
00:07:13,866 --> 00:07:15,034
But as of now,

186
00:07:15,301 --> 00:07:18,387
no solution is truly perfect.

187
00:07:18,387 --> 00:07:20,623
New ideas are constantly emerging,

188
00:07:20,623 --> 00:07:22,291
so I won't go into detail here.

189
00:07:22,291 --> 00:07:25,161
But ultimately, RAG is this kind of structure.

190
00:07:25,228 --> 00:07:27,430
I think it's more like a compromise.

191
00:07:27,430 --> 00:07:30,333
Given the limited context window of large models,

192
00:07:30,333 --> 00:07:31,634
we use this approach

193
00:07:31,634 --> 00:07:34,270
to try and keep the model's answers from being too far off.

194
00:07:34,287 --> 00:07:36,189
It can solve some problems,

195
00:07:36,189 --> 00:07:39,192
but there are indeed many problems it can't solve.

196
00:07:39,192 --> 00:07:40,626
So I'm also waiting for

197
00:07:40,626 --> 00:07:44,397
the next-generation architecture that truly transcends this compromise.

198
00:07:47,867 --> 00:07:49,535
RAG is essentially a form of compression.

199
00:07:50,286 --> 00:07:51,487
The document is too long,

200
00:07:51,487 --> 00:07:54,790
so we chunk it, filter it, build an index.

201
00:07:54,790 --> 00:07:55,992
Keep what's important,

202
00:07:56,025 --> 00:07:58,461
and discard what's not.

203
00:07:58,461 --> 00:08:00,963
Actually, our daily lives are like this too.

204
00:08:00,963 --> 00:08:03,199
Every day we face so many people,

205
00:08:03,266 --> 00:08:04,333
so many things,

206
00:08:04,333 --> 00:08:07,436
and we gradually learn to categorize and make trade-offs.

207
00:08:07,436 --> 00:08:09,755
"This is urgent, that can wait."

208
00:08:09,755 --> 00:08:10,890
"This needs to be remembered,"

209
00:08:10,890 --> 00:08:13,626
while those other things can perhaps be forgotten.

210
00:08:13,626 --> 00:08:16,095
We think this is maturity, clarity of mind.

211
00:08:16,095 --> 00:08:18,865
But sometimes, when things are chunked too finely,

212
00:08:18,865 --> 00:08:22,335
it's inevitable to lose context and misunderstand the original meaning.

213
00:08:22,335 --> 00:08:26,489
Some things that were once important might lose their connection.

214
00:08:26,489 --> 00:08:28,191
Like that old deskmate,

215
00:08:28,191 --> 00:08:32,428
or that close friend whose last message to you was just "lol."

216
00:08:32,428 --> 00:08:34,397
It's not that they've become unimportant;

217
00:08:34,397 --> 00:08:38,401
it's just that the vector coordinates of our respective lives are drifting further and further apart.

218
00:08:38,568 --> 00:08:43,122
A few days ago, I stumbled upon an old screenshot of us playing games together.

219
00:08:43,122 --> 00:08:45,124
I stared at them for a long time.

220
00:08:45,124 --> 00:08:46,726
And suddenly, I realized,

221
00:08:46,726 --> 00:08:48,661
back then,

222
00:08:48,661 --> 00:08:50,663
his DPS was really low!

223
00:08:50,663 --> 00:08:51,998
This is programmer Wang.

224
00:08:52,131 --> 00:08:53,032
See you next time.